A||||9||Fastback Formula||Formula with a fastback?||||5
Z||000000||Fastback Formula||11-01-1999||01:16 AM||||Last August, I started looking for a first car--I'm a junior in high school--and was lucky enough to cross paths with a red Fiero Formula in fair shape with the manual transmission.  It had 95,000 miles, a strong running engine, a well kept interior, but needed a paint job.  After one look, hearing the throaty growl from under the deck, and a short test drive, I fell in love and bought the car the following day.<P>So far, after 1500 miles (we evacuated our home in Jacksonville, FL due to hurricane Floyd, and I wasn't about to leave my pride & joy behind), I'm still impressed with how fun this car is to drive.  Unfortunately, I have had to replace a few parts (with the help of my father, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)--the distributor, throttle position sensor, brake pads (rotors were still in good shape), front brake hoses, and rear emergency brake cables.  Comparing this car to others in its price range ($2500 US), I think I made out well even with these replacement parts.<P>In short (yeah, right... like I type anything short!), I would like to add the GT fastback clip, deck lid, and tail lights to my Formula while keeping the Formula's rear bumper.  I've seen pictures at  <A HREF="http://www.cjnetworks.com/~bott/fom/fmnthapril.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cjnetworks.com/~bott/fom/fmnthapril.html</A>  with this modification, and thought it looked astonishing.  The car at that site had the sleek look of the fastback, while still retaining its Formula identity.  Very cool...<P>I'm wondering how difficult would this be to do.  What parts would be needed from a donor car?  Would I be able to find a GT at a bone-yard and remove the required parts there, or would I be better off watching out for a tired GT for a parts car?  Should I obtain panels painted the same red, or will painting other panels be just as easy. (The entire car needs a paint job anyway)  Do y'all think it's a bad idea to make a hybrid Fiero out of a somewhat rare Formula?<P>Any input on this would be greatly appreciated.  So far, I've been reading many of the posts on this forum, and, to say the least, I am extremely impressed in this virtual Fiero community.  Not a single flame war, people express their opinions which are welcomed by those with differing thoughts, and there are so many knowledgeable people here eager to help others.  I think this might be as good as it gets...||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||11-01-1999||01:44 AM||||About the online flame wars part; you obviously havn't been on the fiero.net mailing list have you?  All it is is flame wars, bullshit, and complaining!  This board is the best resource yet.  <P> On the changing the formula look part;<BR>It's your car, go ahead and do as you please with it.  We are not talking about a '68 Yenko Z28 here, it's a fiero.  And anything you do will probably be easily converted back to original condition.  Personally, I would keep the formula look.  It is very unique.  I love the fastback look (own an '87 GT), but the notchback looks is classic.  It is very distinct.  <P>I am glad you wen't with a manual car too!  I had my '84 SC auto as my first car.  It WAS a great first car, but I wouldn't be selling it right now if it was a manual.  ||||reg||7
Z||000002||fiero56||11-01-1999||07:48 AM||||I agree,<BR>It's your car, you can do with it as you wish.  I met a guy in my neigborhood with a Formula a few months ago.  He had the notchback rear end, but had the aero nose.  Looks pretty cool.  Said the guy he bought it from did that, and he prefered the regular coupe nose like mine.  If you like the way the fastback looks, and I must say I do, then do to it what you want.  Just have fun with your car and enjoy it.||||reg||10
Z||000003||DJRice||11-01-1999||08:57 AM||||Where in Jax do you live?  I used to live up there but I moved down to Titusville after graduating UNF.  I can tell you that any Fieros you see in the boneyards on the Northside are mostly notchbacks and pretty well picked over.  <P>But I happen to know of a deal.   <A HREF="http://www.jaxfin.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jaxfin.com</A>  or email rich@jaxfin.com because he has an 86 GT Fastback for sale for $500 as a parts car.  I think it might be missing some pieces, but you'll have look at it yourself.  The dude is moving and wants to lighten his fiero load.  He has 4.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GT||11-01-1999||11:44 AM||||I thought I was the only one living in Florida on this forum.  This is cool!  Reguarding your formula, in my opinion, I would like to see it stay a notchback.  The reason why I say that is because there are not very many original formulas out there anymore.  But, its your car you do what makes you happy.  see ya.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||11-01-1999||09:54 PM||||Congrats on your "acquisition". Sounds like a great ride! The fastback will fit. All of the Fiero space frames are the same, once you remove the plastic. A similar swap is detailed at <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net/showroom/fastback.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.net/showroom/fastback.html</A>       <BR>BTW... I remember reading in a magazine (one of the Fiero club mags -don't remember which), that there were actually four fastback Formulas produced. Seems that, at the end of the production run, they had four unfinished Formulas, and no notchback rear sections. The line workers said "why not?" When GM found out, they supposedly ordered that the cars be destroyed, but according to the article, at least one "escaped." Urban Legend stuff. <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||Cooter||11-01-1999||10:16 PM||||I converted my 85 SE into a fastback from a used up 86 GT. It really improved the looks of my car, but it is a car that will never be worth much, so the value was not affected by the conversion. I have been looking for an 88 Formula for about 6 months now. I would be more inclined to keep it a notchback. The reason is that the fastback clip looks out of place without the aero bumpers and ground effects that come on the GT. You can see my car at Batboys website to see what I mean.||||reg||10
Z||000007||Fastback Formula||11-02-1999||01:27 AM||||Thanks for the replies so far!  They've been encouraging.<P>I'd love to pick up a parts car, but unfortunately I'm living in an appartment at the moment in the Westside. (just moved here from Western NY)  We're starting to look at houses now, and hopefully we'll find one soon with a decent yard.  If I find a killer deal beforehand, though, I think I'll be able to squeeze the car into the driveway and park behind it.<P>In the near future, I'd like to find a Fiero GT for a parts car--then I'll have the option of swapping panels and I'll have an engine to start rebuilding.  I've been looking at some of the more exotic engine options (Northstar, V8, 3800, Quad 4, 3.4l, ...), but since this would be my first rebuild and engine swap, I think I'll have my hands full with the stock 2.8.<P>I looked at your car Cooter, and that is exactly what I was thinking of.  For some reason, I love the fastback look without the GT fascias and ground effects, although the GT looks pretty sweet, too!  Is your rear fascia from the GT donor car?  If it is, would it be possible to keep the rear fascia from a non-GT?  I believe this is how the car at the first link was done.  Also, was there anything out of the ordinary that you noticed while doing the swap?  Is it just panels, brake lights, and the deck that need to be swapped?<P>I'm still divided in the decision to keep the Formula look or to add the fastback look.  If I find a GT parts car, I'll probably give the fastback a try knowing that I'll be able to revert back to the notchback with little trouble.<P>DJRice, thanks for the info on the parts car.  I'll have some spare time later this week so I'll toss that guy an email.  I'll also start buying Autotraders again.  Do they offer subscriptions?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I checked out the fastback swap, thanks for the link.  It was cool to see how another person managed to do the swap.  It was well documented and fun to read.<P>Later, and thanks for the help so far!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000008||Cooter||11-02-1999||07:54 PM||||The swap of the body panels was not hard. I practically did it by myself in two afternoons. The only problem doing the swap is the tail lights. The coupe has the all red tail lights with the brake and tail lights together. The GT has seperate yellow turn signals from the red brake lights. The entire wiring harness is different. THe GT rear harness will plug into the coupe at the junction at the battery, but IT WON'T WORK! The cooling fan, dash lights and other stuff goes crazy. I modified the existing harness and spliced in the turn signal bulbs to the brake lights so now when you hit the brakes, both the yellow and red come on and they both blink with the turn signals. It is really bright back there! All the bolts in the roof panel need to be removed, but the outer skin can be picked up high enough to remove the rear clip with out completly removing the roof. (just be sure to take out your sunroof if it has it) There were a few bolts that were "hiding", but it was not a bad swap. Do yourself a favor and if you need new GT quarter windows, do not get the fiberglass scoupes like I have. They make a really bad blind spot and lane changes get pretty tough. If you want, I can E-mail you some pic's of the donor car and you will see how the clip is attached.<P>Oh, the rear cover and muffler is from an 85 GT. It had the correct dual exhaust and ribbed moulding that the 86 parts car did not have. Yes, you can use any bumper cover with the rear clip.<P>Good Luck!<P>Greg||||reg||14
Z||000009||GREENDANO||11-05-1999||09:54 PM||||Dan-O is back after a long and still continuing work session.....<BR>  If anyone is interested I can send you pictures of my 85 GT that has been converted to a fastback with a PISA GTFX conversion.  It looks very much like a fastback.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||3
